course: dictionary
lesson: 11944
title: Capped: one word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. «Capped» is a good example. The words around it show you which meaning the writer wants, so check the context before you decide.
explain: «The mountains were snow-capped in winter.» Snow sits on the top of the mountains, so capped means covered at the top, like a cap on a head.
explain: «Your phone bill is capped at £20 a month.» Here capped means it has a top limit, so the bill can never go above £20. Ask yourself: is the word about covering something, or about a limit?
explain: In UK sport, «She was capped 50 times for England» means she has played 50 matches for her country. The words team and country are the clues.
fact: Capped can be an adjective, which describes a noun such as mountains or prices.
fact: Capped is also the past form of the verb cap, which means to put a top on something.
fact: «Snow-capped» means covered at the top with snow.
fact: «Capped» can mean having a top limit put on it, as in «prices are capped at £5».
fact: In UK sport, «capped» means chosen to play for your national team.
exercise: «The hills were snow-capped all winter.» What does snow-capped mean? | *Covered with snow on the top | Very cold and wet all the way through | Shaped like a small hat | Lost somewhere in the snow
exercise: «Your phone bill is capped at £20 a month.» What does capped mean here? | Made of gold | *Has a top limit put on it | Covered with a hat | Paid in full today
exercise: «The bottle was capped and put away.» Which word is a past form of cap? | put | *capped | bottle | away
test: «She was capped for England after a brilliant season.» What does capped mean in this sentence? | Covered with paint | *Chosen to play for her national team | Told to stop | Given a warm coat
test: «The dentist capped my tooth to protect it.» What does capped mean here? | Pulled out completely | Made smaller and shorter | *Covered over with an artificial crown | Painted white
exam: Which sentence uses capped to mean has a top limit? | «The bottle was capped and put away.» | «The hat was capped with a red band.» | *«Ticket prices are capped at £30.» | «The mountain was snow-capped.»
